      "In a randomized trial of 309 hospitalized patients aged 80 and over, facilitated advance care planning — reflecting on values, naming a surrogate and documenting wishes — meant that among those who died within six months, wishes were known and followed in 86% of the intervention group versus 30% of controls, and bereaved relatives had less stress, anxiety and depression. Expert consensus now defines advance care planning as an ongoing values conversation rather than a form, and trials of tailored outreach increase completion of multiple planning steps. The boundary: benefits for caregivers are better established than effects on 'goal-concordant care', which is hard to measure.",
    action: "If a parent is over 75 or has a serious illness, ask their primary clinician or health system whether a facilitated advance care planning conversation is available, attend it with your parent if invited, and make sure the surrogate designation and the document end up in the chart and in your own files.",
